  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Patrick Malouda confides that he too was once a promising chef until an injury ended his kitchen career, and he agrees to return in the evening so Viktor can cook for him. Outside, a bitter Nikita confronts Denis after the canceled wedding.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Поверьте, я знаю, каково это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Когда-то я тоже был поваром.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Из-за травмы пришлось оставить профессию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "К сожалению, такова наша жизнь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, вы ещё раз придёте?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Виктор обязательно сегодня вас удивит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Да, приду с большим удовольствием.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Вы с Катей из-за меня расстались?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел, чтобы вы поженились.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Да, ты очень сильно старался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Особенно тогда вечером на крыше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Я навёл о тебе справки.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Поверьте, я знаю, каково это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Believe me, I know what that is like.' The grammar packs 'believe' and 'this' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Когда-то я тоже был поваром.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Once I too was a chef.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'once' before landing on 'as a cook'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Из-за травмы пришлось оставить профессию.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because of an injury I had to leave the profession.' The meaning hinges on 'because of' working together with 'profession' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "К сожалению, такова наша жизнь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Unfortunately, such is our life.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'regret' and finishing on 'life'. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, вы ещё раз придёте?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe you will come to us once more?' In Russian, the idea of 'maybe' flows naturally into 'to come' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Виктор обязательно сегодня вас удивит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Viktor will definitely surprise you today.' In Russian, the idea of 'definitely' flows naturally into 'to surprise' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "inspector",
      "t": "Да, приду с большим удовольствием.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, I will come with great pleasure.' the inspector brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'will come' in a few short words. Nikita answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Вы с Катей из-за меня расстались?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you and Katya break up because of me?' Nikita anchors the line on 'you', while 'broke up' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я хотел, чтобы вы поженились.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I wanted the two of you to get married.' The grammar packs 'I' and 'got married' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Да, ты очень сильно старался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, you tried very hard.' Nikita anchors the line on 'yes', while 'tried' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Особенно тогда вечером на крыше.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Especially that evening on the roof.' In Russian, the idea of 'especially' flows naturally into 'roof' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Я навёл о тебе справки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I made inquiries about you.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'inquiries'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
